2026-10-10T14:45:00Z  NOTE      measured before writing any budget, by pulling real commands out of `ninja -t commands` and running each under /usr/bin/time -l. Largest-TU compile (src/qml_bridge/SunoBridge.cpp, 1662 LOC + Qt): 306 MiB peak RSS at -O0 -g0 (30s wall, 17s CPU), 301 MiB at -O3 (18s), 338 MiB under RelWithDebInfo -fsanitize=thread (25s). Link of tests/unit/unit_tests (36 objects + Qt frameworks + projectM static): 160 MiB, 1.3s. unit_tests full run: 114 MiB peak RSS and only 14s of CPU across a 91s wall clock. So compiles are CPU-bound and the tests are not -- the suite is idle ~84% of its life. That asymmetry is why ctest is budgeted separately (-j2) while compiles stay at 2. Per-job budget is 512 MiB: the measured worst case plus headroom, deliberately NOT scaled per profile because sanitizer cost lands at RUN time.
2026-10-10T14:45:00Z  NOTE      the machine is 2 physical cores / 4 hyperthreads, 8 GB, 776 MB already swapped. The old default (getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN minus one) asked for 3 jobs on a machine with two real cores and ignored RAM entirely. New rule: 75% of PHYSICAL cores, floored at the physical core count so a 2-core box cannot arithmetic itself down to one serial job, then capped independently by 75% of AVAILABLE (not total) RAM -- total says nothing about whether the desktop is already paging.
2026-10-10T14:45:00Z  NOTE      inxi is installed but deliberately unused: it reports memory as N/A on macOS (it wants dmidecode) and returns empty output silently, which would have left the cap reading total RAM forever. sysctl/vm_stat on Darwin and /proc on Linux cover both with nothing to install. Two bugs were introduced and caught by testing rather than review: --nice/--no-nice parsed then had their value overwritten because the default was assigned AFTER the option parser (moved above it), and --help died on `set -u` for the same reason because usage() interpolates CPU_PERCENT/MEM_PERCENT/PER_JOB_MIB and runs inside the parser.
2026-10-10T14:45:00Z  NOTE      found en route, not in the original scope: moving this machine from brew to port left all four build dirs configured by the now-removed /usr/local/bin/cmake, so every profile failed with FAILED: [code=127]. The obvious cache-based fix (CMAKE_COMMAND in CMakeCache.txt) is wrong and was hit in the act -- a failed reconfigure rewrites that variable BEFORE it errors, so the cache claims a cmake that exists while the 166 commands in build.ninja still name the dead one. The check reads build.ninja for the binary in front of --regenerate-during-build, and tests existence only so two co-installed cmakes cannot ping-pong a reconfigure loop.
